{"name":"Café de Paris","hex":"#889944","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"café","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coffee shop; an establishment selling coffee and sometimes other non-alcoholic beverages, simple meals or snacks, with a facility to consume them on the premises.","A small restaurant of any genre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caf%C3%A9"},{"term":"paris","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of pari."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paris"}],"entries":[{"title":"Café de Paris, London","blocks":[{"text":"The Café de Paris was a nightclub in the West End of London, active from 1924–41 and 1948–2020. It was located on Coventry Street, which runs between Leicester Square and Piccadilly Circus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris_-_London_-_08.JPG/330px-Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris_-_London_-_08.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_London"},{"title":"Lincoln Gardens","blocks":[{"text":"Lincoln Gardens, also known during its history as Royal Gardens, Royal Gardens Café, the New Charleston Café and Café de Paris, was a night club and dance hall that played an instrumental role in the history of jazz and youth culture in the city of Chicago during the first three decades of the 20th century. Opening sometime in the first few years of the 20th century, it was the largest dance hall in South Side, Chicago prior to the opening of the Savoy Ballroom in 1927. Originally a venue that employed only white musicians and catered to only white patrons, it was reinvented as a space for patrons and musicians of all races by African-American entrepreneurs Virgil Williams and William Bottoms in 1918. This reinvented venue was at first the home of Bill Johnson and the Original Creole Band. Later King Oliver's Creole Jazz Band was the resident band at the venue from 1922 through 1924. It closed in June 1927 after the establishment was bombed."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Gardens"},{"title":"Café de Paris (Monaco)","blocks":[{"text":"Café de Paris is a coffeehouse and restaurant in the Belle Époque style of the early 20th century, located in Monte Carlo next to the Casino de Monte-Carlo, on the Place du Casino, Hôtel de Paris. It is owned and managed by the Société des bains de mer de Monaco."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris.JPG/330px-Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris_(Monaco)"},{"title":"Coffeehouse","blocks":[{"text":"A coffeehouse, coffee shop, or café, is an establishment that serves various types of coffee drinks like espresso, latte, americano and cappuccino, as well as other beverages. An espresso bar specializes in serving espresso and espresso-based drinks. Some coffeehouses may serve iced coffee among other cold drinks, as well as non-caffeinated drinks. A coffeehouse may also serve food, such as light snacks, sandwiches, muffins, cakes, breads, pastries or doughnuts. Many doughnut shops in Canada and the U.S. serve coffee to accompany doughnuts, so these can also be classified as coffee shops, although doughnut shops tend to be more casual and serve cheaper fare. In continental Europe, some cafés even serve alcoholic drinks, and in West Asia, coffeehouses may offer a flavored tobacco smoked through a hookah, called shisha in most varieties of Arabic or nargile in Levantine Arabic, Greek, and Turkish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Caf%C3%A9_de_Flore.jpg/330px-Caf%C3%A9_de_Flore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffeehouse"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_%285615896495%29.jpg/250px-Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_%285615896495%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_%285615896495%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_%285615896495%29.jpg/250px-Caf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_%285615896495%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Café de Paris, Monte Carlo, in April 1899","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaf%C3%A9_de_Paris%2C_Monte-Carlo%2C_avril_1899_(5615896495).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg/500px-Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg/1280px-Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg/500px-Cafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Café de Paris, 2014","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACafe_Paris_-_Casino_de_Monte_Carlo_-_Monaco_2014.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Palestinian_women_grinding_coffee_beans.jpg/500px-Palestinian_women_grinding_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip